import json,random
from pathlib import Path
out=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];bank=[];concepts=[]
titles=['Language: nature and functions','Branches of linguistics','Phonetics and phonology','Morphology and grammatical units','Grammar and linguistic analysis','Syntax','Semantics','Pragmatics']
# Fields: term | definition | diagnostic example | misconception | qualification or distinction.
def add(lecture, text):
 for line in text.strip().splitlines():
  if not line.strip():continue
  a=line.split('|')
  assert len(a)==5,(lecture,line)
  concepts.append(dict(lecture=lecture,term=a[0].strip(),definition=a[1].strip(),example=a[2].strip(),false=a[3].strip(),note=a[4].strip()))
add(1, '''
Arbitrariness | A conventional relationship between a linguistic form and its meaning, without a necessary natural resemblance. | English dog and Urdu kutta name the same kind of animal using different sound sequences. | Every word must physically resemble what it denotes. | Arbitrariness permits conventional variation across languages; it does not mean speakers choose meanings randomly during each conversation.
Productivity | The capacity to create and understand new messages by combining familiar linguistic resources. | A listener understands a new sentence about a purple robot teaching cricket on Mars. | A language contains only a fixed list of memorized complete messages. | Productivity concerns new combinations, not simply the number of words already stored.
Displacement | The capacity to communicate about entities or events beyond the immediate situation. | A speaker discusses a holiday planned for next year. | Language can refer only to objects visible at the moment of speaking. | References to the past, future, absent places and imaginary worlds illustrate displacement.
Duality of patterning | Organization at two levels, where individually meaningless elements combine into meaningful units. | The sounds /k/, /æ/ and /t/ combine as cat, while another arrangement produces tack. | Every individual speech sound must have an independent lexical meaning. | Duality distinguishes the level of sound organization from the level of meaningful units.
Discreteness | The organization of language into distinguishable units or categories. | Changing /p/ to /b/ distinguishes pat from bat rather than creating a continuous halfway word. | Linguistic categories are defined only by continuous changes in loudness. | Physical speech varies continuously, but linguistic analysis identifies categorical contrasts.
Cultural transmission | Learning a particular language through exposure and interaction with its users. | A child raised with Urdu acquires Urdu rather than automatically acquiring the ancestral language of a distant family. | A child's particular language is determined solely by biological ancestry. | Biological capacities support acquisition, while experience determines which language or languages develop.
Interchangeability | The ability of participants to function as both producers and receivers of messages. | Two friends alternate between speaking and listening during a conversation. | One conversational participant can only send messages for life. | Interchangeability concerns communicative roles, not whether two words are synonyms.
Reflexivity | The ability to use language to discuss language itself. | A teacher explains that plural is a grammatical term. | Language cannot be used to describe its own forms. | This design property underlies metalinguistic explanation and grammatical discussion.
Linguistic system | A structured set of interacting patterns governing linguistic units. | English permits black cat but normally not cat black as an ordinary adjective-plus-noun expression. | Any order of familiar words is equally grammatical in a language. | Constraints depend on the language and construction; another language may permit a different order.
Linguistic sign | A conventional pairing of a form with a meaning or concept. | The English form tree is associated with the concept of a woody plant. | A linguistic sign is identical to the physical object it refers to. | The sign and its referent differ: the word tree is not a tree.
Informative function | Using language primarily to communicate propositional information. | A notice states that the library closes at five. | Informative language cannot communicate facts about opening times. | A single utterance can have several functions; this label identifies its main informational purpose.
Interpersonal function | Using language to negotiate relationships, attitudes and social roles. | A student addresses a lecturer as Professor to mark their relationship. | Forms of address never express social relationships. | Interpersonal meaning includes stance and relationship management, and may coexist with factual content.
Performative function | Using an appropriate utterance to carry out a socially recognized act. | An authorized official says I declare the meeting open in the proper setting. | Any speaker can legally marry any couple merely by saying a formula anywhere. | Authority, circumstances and accepted procedures matter for institutional performances.
Emotive function | Using language to express feelings or emotional reactions. | A surprised spectator says Wow, what a finish! | Emotional expressions must always provide a neutral factual report. | Emotive language can express the speaker's state without successfully changing anyone else's emotion.
Phatic communion | Using language chiefly to establish or maintain social contact. | Two neighbours exchange Hello and Nice day while passing. | Every greeting primarily introduces new technical information. | Phatic exchanges help sustain relationships even when little new information is conveyed.
Recreational function | Using language for enjoyment or verbal play. | Friends invent playful rhymes for amusement. | Language used for pleasure must always be a legal declaration. | Jokes, wordplay and playful verse may serve this function, often alongside other purposes.
Metalingual function | Using language to clarify a linguistic code or expression. | A learner asks What does morpheme mean? | Explaining a word's meaning cannot be a use of language. | Metalingual is a function label; reflexivity names the broader capacity making it possible.
Spoken modality | The vocal-auditory channel of linguistic communication. | A speaker produces an oral utterance that another person hears. | All human languages must use the vocal-auditory channel. | Speech is widespread, but natural signed languages use a visual-gestural modality.
Signed language | A natural linguistic system expressed through conventional visual-gestural forms. | Deaf signers combine signs according to the grammar of their community's sign language. | Natural signed languages lack grammar and merely mime spoken words. | Signed languages have productive grammars and are not universal across all communities.
Writing system | Conventional visible symbols used to represent aspects of language. | A learner records an Urdu sentence using the Urdu script. | A language cannot exist until it has a writing system. | Writing is historically later than speech and is not required for a community to have a full language.
Linguistic competence | A speaker's internal knowledge of a linguistic system in a competence-performance distinction. | A speaker recognizes a grammatical sentence they have never heard before. | Competence consists only of flawless pronunciation in public speaking. | In this theoretical distinction, competence concerns knowledge rather than every detail of actual performance.
Linguistic performance | Actual language use under real processing and situational conditions. | A tired speaker makes a slip despite knowing the intended construction. | Every hesitation proves that a speaker lacks grammatical knowledge. | Memory, attention and fatigue can affect performance without eliminating underlying knowledge.
Descriptive orientation | Investigating the patterns people use without treating social prestige as grammatical superiority. | A linguist records the systematic agreement patterns of a regional variety. | Only prestigious accents can have systematic linguistic rules. | Description can recognize social judgments while distinguishing them from structural analysis.
Language versus communication | The distinction between a linguistic system and the broader transmission of information. | Smoke from a fire communicates danger but does not by itself constitute a human language. | Every signal that conveys information is a full natural language. | Communication includes nonlinguistic signals; language is a particular structured capacity and social system.
Scientific study of language | Using evidence and explicit analysis to investigate linguistic structure, use and acquisition. | A researcher tests a hypothesis about word order against recorded examples. | Linguistics consists entirely of listing etiquette rules for correct speech. | Linguistics includes many methods, but claims should be open to evidence and revision.
''')
add(2, '''
Phonetics | The investigation of the physical production, transmission and perception of speech sounds. | A researcher measures tongue position while someone produces [t]. | Phonetics studies only the spelling of words. | Its articulatory, acoustic and auditory perspectives address different aspects of physical speech.
Phonology | The investigation of sound patterns and contrasts in a linguistic system. | A researcher asks whether changing one sound changes a word's meaning in English. | Phonology is simply another name for the physics of sound waves. | Its questions include phonemes, allophones, phonotactics, stress and systematic alternations.
Morphology | The study of the internal structure of words and the patterns forming them. | A researcher separates unhelpful into un-, help and -ful. | Morphology studies only the order of complete independent clauses. | It investigates meaningful or grammatical units inside words and relations among word forms.
Syntax | The study of how words and phrases combine into larger grammatical structures. | An analyst compares The child opened the gate with an unacceptable reordering. | Syntax is the study of vocal-fold vibration. | Syntactic structures are hierarchical rather than merely unordered lists of words.
Semantics | The study of conventional linguistic meaning, including word and sentence meaning. | An analyst explains why rose entails flower in an appropriate lexical sense. | Semantics examines only how loud a speaker sounds. | Context can help select a sense, but semantic analysis addresses encoded meaning and meaning relations.
Pragmatics | The study of interpretation and action in context. | Can you open the window? is understood as a request during a hot lesson. | Pragmatics ignores who is speaking and the situation of an utterance. | It includes implicature, speech acts, deixis and contextual assumptions.
Lexicography | The theory and practice of compiling dictionaries. | An editor chooses definitions, examples and usage labels for dictionary entries. | Lexicography means diagnosing damage to language-related brain networks. | Dictionary making uses linguistic evidence, and modern practice often draws on corpora.
Lexicology | The study of vocabulary and the organization and relations of lexical items. | A scholar examines relations among words in a lexical field. | Lexicology is identical to physically printing a dictionary. | Lexicology investigates the lexicon, while lexicography concerns dictionary theory and practice.
Sociolinguistics | The study of relationships between language and social life or social variation. | A researcher compares pronunciation across social groups and speaking styles. | Sociolinguistics treats age and social setting as irrelevant to language use. | Correlations require evidence and do not justify assuming that all members of a group speak alike.
Dialectology | The study of dialect variation, traditionally including its geographical distribution. | A map compares local words for the same object across districts. | Dialectology studies only changes in a single person's hearing. | Dialects may differ in pronunciation, vocabulary and grammar; accent refers principally to pronunciation.
Psycholinguistics | The study of mental processes involved in language acquisition, comprehension and production. | An experiment measures how quickly listeners recognize a spoken word. | Psycholinguistics concerns only the legal wording of contracts. | Acquisition and disorders are relevant topics, but the field also studies ordinary processing.
Neurolinguistics | The study of neural mechanisms supporting language. | A researcher relates language performance after a brain lesion to neural damage. | Neurolinguistics assumes every language ability lies in one isolated brain point. | Language depends on interacting networks; lesion and imaging evidence require careful interpretation.
Discourse analysis | The investigation of language organization and meaning across stretches of interaction or text. | An analyst studies how turns connect during an interview. | Discourse analysis must ignore relationships between successive utterances. | Its units may exceed a single sentence and include interactional organization and textual interpretation.
Stylistics | Linguistic investigation of choices and patterns associated with effects in texts or speech. | A critic examines how repeated short clauses create urgency in a passage. | Stylistics judges a text without examining any linguistic features. | It connects patterns to interpretations with textual evidence rather than impression alone.
Literary stylistics | Linguistic analysis of literature and its interpretive effects. | A researcher studies foregrounded repetition in a poem. | Literary stylistics excludes syntax and sound from analysis. | It may draw on phonetics, grammar, semantics, pragmatics and discourse analysis.
Applied linguistics | The investigation of real-world problems involving language. | A team evaluates language teaching materials for multilingual learners. | Applied linguistics is limited to correcting punctuation. | Teaching is important, but translation, assessment, policy and other practical problems also fall within its scope.
Computational linguistics | The computational modelling and processing of natural language. | A system is developed to parse Urdu sentences automatically. | Computational linguistics studies Java syntax instead of human language. | Programming is a tool; natural-language structure and processing are central objects of inquiry.
Cognitive linguistics | Approaches relating language to general cognition, conceptualization and experience. | An analyst investigates the conceptual metaphor TIME IS MONEY. | Cognitive linguistics defines language as unrelated to human conceptualization. | It is an approach or family of approaches, not simply the name for all laboratory language experiments.
Anthropological linguistics | The study of language in relation to culture and human social practices. | An ethnographer examines how kinship terms organize social relationships. | Anthropological linguistics investigates only isolated sound waves in a laboratory. | Linguistic anthropology and anthropological linguistics overlap, though institutional emphases differ.
Historical linguistics | The investigation of language change and historical relationships among languages. | A scholar reconstructs earlier forms using systematic correspondences across related languages. | Any two words that sound alike prove that their languages are related. | Genetic relationships require systematic evidence, not isolated resemblance or borrowing alone.
Linguistic typology | The comparison and classification of languages by structural properties. | A study compares SOV and SVO word-order patterns across unrelated languages. | Typology groups languages only by their writing systems. | Structural similarity does not by itself establish descent from a common ancestor.
Corpus linguistics | The systematic study of language using organized collections of authentic language data. | A researcher searches a tagged corpus to compare frequent verb-noun combinations. | Corpus linguistics consists only of collecting files without analyzing them. | Corpus design, sampling, annotation and interpretation affect what conclusions are justified.
Forensic linguistics | Linguistic investigation of language in legal and investigative contexts. | An expert examines how the wording of a police interview affects interpretation. | Forensic linguistics guarantees identification of an author from any short sentence. | Its findings are evidence-based and probabilistic where appropriate, not infallible identification.
Second-language acquisition | The study of learning additional languages beyond initial language acquisition. | A researcher tracks how adult Urdu speakers develop English tense marking. | Second-language acquisition means only compiling bilingual dictionaries. | It examines learning processes and outcomes; it does not imply that only a second language can be studied.
Microlinguistics | A broad teaching label for investigation of language's internal structural systems. | A course groups phonology, morphology and syntax as core internal analyses. | Every textbook must draw exactly the same boundary around microlinguistics. | Micro/macro boundaries vary, especially for pragmatics, and are not universal divisions of nature.
''')
add(3, '''
Articulatory phonetics | The study of how speech organs produce speech sounds. | An investigator tracks lip closure while a person says [p]. | Articulatory phonetics measures only dictionary word frequency. | Articulators include the tongue and lips; the larynx and airflow also matter for production.
Acoustic phonetics | The study of measurable physical properties of speech signals. | An investigator measures frequency and duration from a recorded vowel. | Acoustic phonetics classifies sounds solely by their written letters. | Frequency, amplitude, duration and spectral structure are acoustic properties.
Auditory phonetics | The study of hearing and perception of speech sounds. | Listeners judge whether two recorded consonants sound different. | Auditory phonetics concerns only the motion of the tongue. | The auditory system receives acoustic signals, while perception also involves interpretation.
Pulmonic egressive airflow | Speech airflow powered by air moving out from the lungs. | A person produces an ordinary English [s] while exhaling. | Ordinary English speech is primarily produced by air flowing into the lungs. | Most English speech sounds use this mechanism, although other languages also use nonpulmonic consonants.
Voicing | Vocal-fold vibration during the production of a sound. | The folds vibrate during a sustained [z]. | Voicing is defined as making a sound louder than a whisper. | Compare [z] and [s]: the key contrast is laryngeal vibration rather than loudness.
Bilabial place | Articulation involving both lips. | Both lips come together to produce [p], [b] or [m]. | Bilabial articulation requires the tongue tip against the upper teeth. | Place identifies the articulators; manner distinguishes a stop such as [p] from a nasal such as [m].
Labiodental place | Articulation involving the lower lip and upper teeth. | The lower lip approaches the upper teeth for [f]. | [f] is formed by complete closure of both lips. | English [f] and [v] share place and manner but differ in voicing.
Dental place | Articulation involving the tongue and the teeth. | English [θ] in thin is produced at the teeth. | English [θ] is a bilabial nasal. | Some descriptions call these English sounds interdental; [ð] is the voiced counterpart.
Alveolar place | Articulation at or near the ridge behind the upper teeth. | The tongue contacts the alveolar ridge during [t]. | Alveolar sounds require closure of the vocal folds as their oral place. | English [t], [d], [n], [s], [z] and [l] are commonly classified as alveolar.
Velar place | Articulation involving the tongue dorsum and soft palate. | The back of the tongue makes closure for [k]. | Velar articulation is made with the upper teeth and lower lip. | [k], [ɡ] and [ŋ] share a velar place, though they differ in other properties.
Glottal place | Articulation whose relevant constriction is at the glottis. | The vocal folds close to produce a glottal stop [ʔ]. | A glottal stop requires the lips to seal the mouth. | Glottal describes place in the larynx; it is not a synonym for a velar constriction.
Stop manner | A manner involving complete closure followed by release or another transition. | Air is briefly blocked in the mouth during [p]. | Stops require continuous narrow-channel friction throughout their articulation. | Oral stops contrast with fricatives; not every stop has a strong audible release in every context.
Fricative manner | A manner involving turbulent airflow through a narrow constriction. | A sustained [s] produces audible friction. | A fricative always involves an oral closure that blocks airflow completely. | Fricatives can differ in place and voicing, as in [f], [s] and [z].
Affricate manner | A stop closure followed by a closely coordinated fricative release. | English [tʃ] in church begins with closure and releases into friction. | Every affricate is simply a vowel followed by a nasal. | Affricates often function as single consonant units despite their complex articulation.
Nasal manner | Production with an open nasal passage so air escapes through the nose. | The velum is lowered during [m]. | Nasal consonants require the nasal passage to be completely sealed. | A nasal consonant typically has oral closure alongside nasal airflow.
Approximant manner | Articulation with a constriction that normally does not produce fricative turbulence. | The tongue approaches the palate for [j] in yes. | Every approximant has the same complete closure as [p]. | Glides and liquids are often grouped as approximants, with finer distinctions in their airflow.
Vowel height | The dimension associated with the tongue body's relative high or low position in vowel production. | [i] has a higher tongue position than [a]. | Vowel height is the same as a speaker's habitual loudness. | High/close and low/open are alternative descriptive labels in vowel classification.
Vowel backness | The dimension associated with the tongue body's relative front or back position. | [i] is front while [u] is back. | Backness means how far a written vowel appears from the end of a word. | Height, backness and lip rounding jointly help describe vowel quality.
Lip rounding | The protrusion or rounding of the lips during speech. | A typical [u] is produced with rounded lips. | All vowels in all languages must be rounded. | Rounding is distinct from backness: front rounded vowels occur in many languages.
Diphthong | A vowel with a noticeable quality change within one syllable. | The vowel of many English pronunciations of my glides as [aɪ]. | A diphthong must consist of two separate syllables. | Accent matters: vowel realizations and phonemic analyses differ across English varieties.
Schwa | The mid central vowel represented by [ə], often occurring in unstressed syllables in English. | Many English speakers use [ə] in the first syllable of about. | Schwa is the IPA symbol for a voiceless bilabial stop. | Schwa's distribution varies by language and accent; it is not inherently unstressed in every language.
Phone | A speech sound considered as a physical phonetic realization. | An analyst transcribes an actual aspirated release as [pʰ]. | A phone is a written letter of an alphabet. | Square brackets conventionally mark phonetic transcription.
Phoneme | A contrastive unit in a language's sound system. | Substituting /b/ for /p/ changes pat to bat in English. | Two sounds belong to different phonemes whenever their waveforms differ at all. | Contrast is language-specific; a phoneme is an abstract category rather than a single identical waveform.
Allophone | A contextually or otherwise conditioned realization of a phoneme that does not by itself create a lexical contrast in that system. | English /p/ is aspirated in pin but typically unaspirated after /s/ in spin. | English [pʰ] and [p] must distinguish words in every position. | The same phonetic distinction can be allophonic in one language and phonemic in another.
Minimal pair | Two words differing in one sound in the same position with a difference in meaning. | English sip and zip differ only in their initial consonants. | Two words form a minimal pair whenever they rhyme, regardless of other differences. | Minimal pairs provide strong evidence for phonemic contrast in the language being analyzed.
Complementary distribution | A pattern in which two sounds occur in mutually exclusive environments. | Aspirated and unaspirated English voiceless stops follow predictable environments in a simplified analysis. | Complementary distribution means the sounds occur in exactly the same environments. | Distribution supports an allophonic analysis when phonetic similarity and other evidence also fit.
Broad transcription | A transcription emphasizing contrastive categories while omitting much phonetic detail. | An English word is written with /p/ without marking aspiration. | Broad transcription must record every small articulatory detail. | Slashes commonly mark phonemic transcription; narrow phonetic detail is usually bracketed.
Narrow transcription | A transcription including comparatively detailed phonetic properties. | An analyst writes [pʰ] to mark aspiration explicitly. | Narrow transcription is defined by the use of ordinary spelling alone. | Diacritics can mark aspiration, nasalization and other details relevant to an analysis.
Spectrogram | A display showing how a signal's spectral energy varies over time. | Time runs horizontally and frequency vertically, with shading representing energy. | The vertical axis of a conventional spectrogram represents grammatical tense. | Display settings affect appearance, so darkness is not a universal ranking of all vowels above all consonants.
Formants | Resonant frequency regions of the vocal tract reflected in the speech spectrum. | An analyst compares F1 and F2 to investigate vowel quality. | Formants are identical to the repetition rate of vocal-fold vibration. | Fundamental frequency concerns periodicity; formants concern resonances shaped by the vocal tract.
Fundamental frequency | The repetition rate of a periodic waveform, related in voiced speech to vocal-fold vibration. | A voiced segment has a fundamental frequency measured in hertz. | Fundamental frequency is measured in seconds and means segment duration. | Perceived pitch is related to fundamental frequency, but physical and perceptual measures are not identical.
Stress | Relative prominence assigned to syllables within words or larger structures. | The first syllable is prominent in the English noun REcord. | Stress is determined entirely by which letters are capitalized in normal spelling. | Duration, pitch and intensity can contribute to stress, with language-specific patterns.
Intonation | Patterns of pitch variation over phrases or utterances. | A speaker changes a sentence's pitch contour to convey a particular interactional stance. | Every question in every language must have rising intonation. | Contour interpretation depends on construction, language, variety and context.
Assimilation | A process in which one sound becomes more similar to a nearby sound. | In casual speech, an alveolar nasal may become bilabial before a bilabial consonant. | Assimilation means a sound must become less like its neighbour. | Coarticulatory pressures often motivate assimilation, but the precise patterns are language-specific.
Phonotactics | Constraints on permissible sound sequences and syllable patterns in a language. | English allows /str/ at the start of street but not every imaginable initial cluster. | A cluster impossible in English must be impossible in every language. | Phonotactic restrictions differ across languages and concern patterns beyond individual sound inventories.
''')
add(4, '''
Morpheme | The smallest unit with a meaning or grammatical function in a morphological analysis. | Cats contains cat and a plural marker. | Every morpheme must be an entire written word. | A morpheme may be free or bound; grammatical meaning counts even without a concrete object referent.
Morph | An observable form realizing a morpheme. | The sound [s] realizes the plural morpheme in cats. | A morph is an abstract meaning with no realization. | Morpheme names the analytical unit; morph identifies its realization in a particular form.
Allomorph | An alternative realization of the same morpheme. | English regular plurals have [s], [z] and [ɪz] realizations. | Alternative plural allomorphs must express different grammatical numbers. | Allomorphs preserve the relevant grammatical contribution despite differences in form.
Free morpheme | A morpheme capable of occurring as a word on its own. | Book can occur alone and also inside books. | A free morpheme must always attach to another morpheme. | A complex word such as duster is not a single free morpheme merely because it can stand alone.
Bound morpheme | A morpheme that cannot ordinarily occur independently as a word. | The past-tense suffix -ed attaches to a verbal base. | Every bound morpheme is a complete independent sentence. | Affixes are bound, but bound roots also occur, so bound morpheme and affix are not exact synonyms.
Lexical morpheme | A morpheme contributing substantial content meaning. | The root tiger contributes the animal concept in tigers. | Lexical morphemes are restricted to articles and conjunctions. | Lexical roots often belong to open classes such as nouns, verbs and adjectives.
Functional morpheme | A morpheme contributing grammatical organization or relational information. | The article the marks a noun phrase as definite in a suitable context. | Functional morphemes have no grammatical contribution at all. | The category includes free function words and, in broader usage, bound grammatical markers.
Derivation | Morphology forming a new lexeme or a new lexical meaning, sometimes changing word class. | Adding un- to happy creates unhappy. | Derivation must always change the grammatical category of its base. | Unhappy remains an adjective; category preservation does not prevent an operation from being derivational.
Inflection | Morphology expressing grammatical contrasts within a lexeme's paradigm. | Walk and walked express different grammatical forms of the same verb. | English past-tense inflection always creates a new noun lexeme. | Inflection generally preserves lexical category and core lexical identity.
Prefix | An affix attached before its base. | Re- precedes write in rewrite. | A prefix must occur after the root. | Position defines a prefix; whether it is derivational or inflectional depends on its function in the language.
Suffix | An affix attached after its base. | -ness follows kind in kindness. | A suffix must be inserted in the middle of a root. | English suffixes can be derivational or inflectional, as -ness and plural -s illustrate.
Infix | An affix inserted within a root or base. | Tagalog sulat and sumulat illustrate an inserted -um-. | The English plural men is formed by inserting an ordinary infix into man. | Men illustrates internal vowel change; genuine infixation requires an inserted morphological element.
Root | The basic lexical core remaining after relevant affixes are removed. | Help is the root in unhelpfulness. | A root is always the final inflectional ending of a word. | Roots can be free or bound; not every root can occur as an independent word.
Base | A form to which a morphological operation applies. | Helpful is the base to which un- attaches in unhelpful. | A base must contain exactly one morpheme. | A base may be a root or an already complex form.
Stem | A form to which an inflectional operation applies in a common introductory usage. | Worker is the stem receiving plural -s in workers. | A stem can never contain a derivational suffix. | Stem and base terminology varies somewhat across frameworks; the intended definition should be stated.
Regular plural allomorphy | Phonologically conditioned realization of the regular English plural ending. | Dogs ends in [z], cats in [s], and buses in [ɪz] in many descriptions. | The plural ending in dogs is normally the same voiceless [s] as in cats. | A sibilant requires the syllabic variant; otherwise the preceding segment's voicing normally conditions the ending.
Regular past allomorphy | Phonologically conditioned realization of the regular English past-tense ending. | Walked has [t], played [d], and wanted [ɪd] in common transcriptions. | Every written -ed ending necessarily forms a separate syllable. | The syllabic variant follows /t/ or /d/; other environments condition [t] or [d].
Suppletion | A morphological relation involving forms with substantially different roots. | Go and went are related as present and past forms of one verb. | Suppletion is merely adding regular -ed to every verb. | The relation is grammatical despite the absence of an ordinary predictable shared root shape.
Zero plural | A plural form without an overt plural affix in an appropriate analysis. | One sheep and three sheep share the same overt noun form. | A noun without a visible suffix can never be plural. | Number can be identified from syntax and context even when the noun has no overt plural marker.
Internal vowel change | A morphological pattern realized by changing a vowel inside a base. | Sing, sang and sung differ in their internal vowels. | Internal vowel change requires a detachable infix in every case. | Internal modification differs from concatenating a separate prefix or suffix.
Compounding | Formation of a word from two or more lexical bases. | Toothbrush combines tooth and brush. | A compound must be written as one uninterrupted string in every case. | Spacing and hyphenation do not reliably determine compound status on their own.
Conversion | A change in lexical category without an overt derivational affix. | The noun bottle is used as a verb in They bottle the juice. | Conversion always requires the suffix -ness. | Also called zero derivation in some accounts, this process requires attention to actual use and analysis.
Morpheme count | The number of meaningful or grammatical units identified in a word. | Unhelpful contains three units: un-, help and -ful. | The number of morphemes must equal the number of syllables. | Teacher has two morphemes, while some multisyllabic words such as banana are monomorphemic.
Derivation before inflection | A common English ordering in which derivation is nearer the root than outer inflection. | Workers has work + -er + plural -s. | Workers is normally built by adding agentive -er outside already plural works. | Morphological hierarchy matters; this English tendency should not be universalized without qualification.
Noun phrase | A phrase headed by a noun or nominal element in a traditional introductory analysis. | The very old house functions as subject in The very old house collapsed. | A noun phrase must always contain at least two written words. | A single noun or pronoun can form a phrase; determiner-phrase analyses provide a different theoretical treatment.
Prepositional phrase | A phrase headed by a preposition, often with a complement. | Under the table contains the preposition under and its complement. | A phrase headed by under becomes an adjective phrase merely because it modifies a noun. | Phrase category follows the head; grammatical function and phrase category must be distinguished.
Adjective phrase | A phrase headed by an adjective. | Very proud of her work is headed by proud. | Nice blue shirt is an adjective phrase headed by shirt. | Nice blue shirt is a noun phrase; adjective modifiers do not change its nominal head.
Adverb phrase | A phrase headed by an adverb. | Quite slowly is headed by slowly. | In a loud voice is an adverb phrase by head category. | In a loud voice is a prepositional phrase that may function as an adverbial.
Verb phrase | A phrase headed by a verb, with its dependents according to the framework used. | Has eaten the apple includes a verbal head and its object in a common syntactic analysis. | Every account limits a verb phrase to auxiliaries and excludes objects. | Some school grammars use a narrower verb-group label; state the adopted framework when boundaries matter.
Infinitival construction | A nonfinite construction based on an infinitive verb. | To read novels functions as the complement of likes in She likes to read novels. | Infinitival to always functions as a preposition taking a noun object. | Many modern grammars treat such units as nonfinite clauses rather than simply infinitive phrases.
Gerund construction | A nonfinite -ing construction traditionally associated with a nominal function. | Reading novels is relaxing places reading novels in subject position. | Every word ending in -ing is a gerund functioning as a noun. | Compare a progressive verb in is reading; modern analyses often use a unified gerund-participial category.
Participial modifier | A participial construction modifying a nominal expression or supplying supplementary information. | The child wearing a red hat waved uses wearing a red hat to modify child. | Every participial modifier must be enclosed in commas. | Restrictive modifiers often lack commas; supplementary participial constructions are commonly set off.
Absolute construction | A supplementary construction with its own subject-like element, often without a finite verb. | Her eyes on the clock, Sara waited for the bus. | An absolute construction must always contain a finite past-tense verb. | The construction comments on the accompanying situation; her eyes and on the clock form its internal relation.
Independent clause | A clause capable of functioning as a complete sentence in ordinary use. | Tara ate a cheese roll can stand independently. | Every clause introduced by although is independent. | Independence concerns syntactic status, not whether a reader needs any wider context to understand a reference.
Dependent clause | A clause syntactically subordinate to another construction. | After she watched the news is subordinate in Tara ate after she watched the news. | A dependent clause must lack a verb. | Finite subordinate clauses often have an overt subject and verb, while nonfinite clauses may omit an overt subject.
''')
add(5, '''
Mental grammar | Internalized linguistic knowledge supporting a speaker's patterns and judgments. | A child uses word-order patterns without being able to state a school grammar rule. | Mental grammar exists only after a child has completed a grammar textbook. | Implicit knowledge is distinct from conscious terminology and explicit classroom instruction.
Descriptive grammar | An account of the patterns actually found in a language or variety. | A grammar records a community's consistent use of multiple negation. | A descriptive grammar must condemn every nonstandard construction as structureless. | A nonstandard pattern can be systematic even when institutions discourage it.
Prescriptive grammar | Norms recommending which forms people should use in particular contexts. | A style manual recommends a particular form for formal examination writing. | Prescriptive advice is automatically a complete account of all native-speaker usage. | Prescriptions may be useful for a register, but they differ from a description of every variety.
Pedagogical grammar | A grammar organized to support language teaching and learning. | A textbook explains English question formation with practice exercises. | A pedagogical grammar must assume learners already know every language rule explicitly. | It may simplify or sequence material, and need not compare the target language with a first language in every lesson.
Universal Grammar | A theoretical proposal about innate constraints or capacities specific to human language in generative approaches. | A generative account asks which aspects of possible grammars are biologically constrained. | Universal Grammar is a proven list of identical surface sentences in all languages. | The hypothesis and its scope are debated; it is not identical to any observed cross-linguistic generalization.
Grammaticality | Conformity to the structural patterns of a specified language variety in an analysis. | The children are playing conforms to standard English agreement. | A sentence becomes ungrammatical merely because it describes an imaginary event. | Grammaticality differs from truth, plausibility, politeness and stylistic preference.
Acceptability | Speakers' judgments of how natural or usable an expression seems in context. | A highly nested sentence feels difficult despite following a proposed rule system. | Acceptability is unaffected by memory, context or processing demands. | Acceptability can reflect grammatical knowledge together with processing and contextual factors.
Competence-performance distinction | A theoretical separation of linguistic knowledge from its actual use. | A speaker knows a construction but produces a tired slip while saying it. | A single speech error necessarily describes the full grammar of a community. | Performance data are evidence for competence, but interpretation must consider processing conditions.
Lexicon | The inventory of lexical knowledge, including information about words and morphemes. | A speaker knows that give typically permits a recipient and a theme. | The mental lexicon contains only spelling and never grammatical information. | Pronunciation, meaning and combinatorial properties may be part of lexical knowledge.
Phonetic Form | The sound-related interface representation in generative models using the PF label. | A derivation is interpreted on its pronunciation side at PF in a simplified model. | PF is the abbreviation for pragmatic function in the lecture's generative model. | PF is a theoretical representation, not simply the visible written form of a sentence.
Logical Form | A meaning-related interface representation in generative models using the LF label. | A model uses LF to represent aspects of interpretation such as scope. | LF means the physical waveform recorded by a microphone. | PF/LF terminology belongs to particular frameworks and should not be presented as a complete theory-neutral description.
Language acquisition | Development of a linguistic system through experience and learning. | A child develops productive word combinations through interaction. | Every child follows an identical timetable and produces the same first words. | Broad developmental patterns coexist with substantial individual, linguistic and environmental variation.
Overregularization | Application of a productive pattern to a form that is irregular in adult usage. | A child says goed where the adult form is went. | A child's use of goed shows only exact imitation of adult went. | The error provides evidence for pattern generalization alongside learning of exceptional forms.
Implicit knowledge | Knowledge expressed in skilled use without necessarily being consciously verbalized. | A fluent speaker chooses a natural word order but cannot explain the rule. | Implicit linguistic knowledge requires memorizing the name of every grammatical category. | Explicit rule knowledge and fluent implicit performance can dissociate.
Explicit knowledge | Consciously accessible knowledge that can be stated or reflected on. | A learner recites the rule for forming a regular English plural. | Being able to recite a rule always guarantees effortless real-time use. | Explicit knowledge can support learning but does not automatically equal fluent performance.
Dialect equality | The linguistic principle that natural dialects are systematic rather than inherently deficient. | A linguist treats regional agreement patterns as rule-governed. | Socially prestigious dialects are inherently more logical in all grammatical respects. | Social opportunities associated with standard varieties are real, but do not establish structural superiority.
Standard variety | A socially established variety used in education, administration or other institutional settings. | A school adopts standard written English for examination essays. | A standard variety is the only variety with a grammar. | Standardization is social and historical; other varieties also have structured linguistic systems.
Idiolect | The linguistic patterns associated with an individual speaker. | One speaker consistently prefers a particular pronunciation and expression. | An idiolect is necessarily a separate national language. | Individual patterns interact with community varieties and change across context and time.
Synchronic analysis | Investigation of a linguistic system at a particular time or period. | A study describes present-day Urdu case marking without tracing its history. | Synchronic analysis must reconstruct several ancestral stages. | A synchronic description can involve variation; it does not mean every speaker uses identical forms.
Diachronic analysis | Investigation of linguistic change through time. | A study traces the loss of certain English inflectional endings across centuries. | Diachronic analysis examines only the current acoustic pitch of a single vowel token. | It concerns historical development, which differs from description at a single period.
Linguistic universal | A proposed property or tendency holding across languages, stated with an appropriate scope. | A typologist tests whether a structural pattern occurs across a diverse language sample. | A pattern observed in two related languages is automatically a universal. | Absolute claims and statistical tendencies require different evidence and should not be conflated.
Language typology | Comparison of structural patterns across languages. | Languages are compared according to their dominant ordering of subject, object and verb. | Two languages with SOV order must belong to the same historical family. | Typological grouping and genetic classification answer different questions.
Corpus evidence | Observations drawn from systematically collected language data. | Repeated corpus examples support the claim that a collocation is frequent in a register. | A construction absent from one small corpus is necessarily impossible in the language. | Sampling and corpus size limit what absence and frequency can show.
Elicitation | Collection of linguistic evidence through targeted tasks or questions to speakers. | A researcher asks speakers to judge alternative sentences in a controlled context. | Elicited judgments never depend on task design or the context supplied. | Elicitation complements naturally occurring data and benefits from multiple speakers and careful controls.
Levels of analysis | Complementary analytical perspectives on different aspects of linguistic organization. | One sentence is examined for sound patterns, word structure, syntax and meaning. | Explaining a sentence's syntax automatically replaces every semantic and pragmatic analysis. | Levels interact but address distinguishable questions; lists of levels vary across teaching frameworks.
''')
add(6, '''
Constituent | A word or group of words functioning as a structural unit within a larger expression. | The old house can be replaced by it in The old house collapsed. | Any arbitrary consecutive sequence of words must form a constituent. | Constituency concerns hierarchical units; substitution, movement and coordination can provide evidence.
Immediate constituents | The units into which a larger structure is divided at its next structural level. | In a simple analysis, The child and laughed are the immediate constituents of The child laughed. | Immediate constituents must always be individual letters. | Immediate units differ from the smallest terminal units reached after further analysis.
Terminal units | The smallest units represented at the leaves of a particular syntactic analysis. | Individual words appear as leaves in a word-level tree of a sentence. | The terminal units of every possible grammatical analysis must be whole sentences. | The chosen level matters; analyses incorporating morphology may decompose words further.
Hierarchical structure | Organization in which smaller units combine into nested larger units. | The noun phrase the child with a hat contains the smaller prepositional phrase with a hat. | Syntactic structure is fully captured by listing words alphabetically. | Hierarchy explains relations that linear order alone cannot capture.
Head | The central element that determines important properties of a phrase. | Proud heads the adjective phrase very proud of her work. | The first written word in every phrase is necessarily its head. | The head's position varies across constructions and languages.
Subject | A grammatical relation associated with properties such as agreement and clause structure. | In The window was broken by Ali, the window is the subject. | The subject must always intentionally perform an action. | Grammatical subject and semantic agent differ, especially in passives and experiencer constructions.
Direct object | A verb-dependent nominal grammatical relation in a transitive construction. | The book is the direct object in Sara read the book. | Every phrase after a verb is a direct object. | A prepositional phrase such as to school is not a direct object merely because it follows a verb.
Subject complement | An expression predicating a property or identity of the subject after a linking verb. | A doctor is the subject complement in Amina is a doctor. | The C in SVC stands for consonant. | Subject complements differ from objects: they characterize or identify the subject.
Object complement | An expression predicating a property or identity of an object. | Happy describes the object him in The news made him happy. | An object complement must identify the grammatical subject instead of the object. | The pattern is often labelled SVOC, with an object plus a predication about it.
Adjunct | A modifier not selected as a required complement in the relevant construction. | Yesterday adds optional time information in Sara smiled yesterday. | Every optional modifier is automatically the verb's direct object. | Optionality is a useful clue but is not a complete universal test of adjunct status.
SV pattern | A clause pattern consisting of a subject and an intransitive verb. | The baby slept is a basic SV clause. | An SV pattern requires an overt direct object. | Additional optional modifiers may occur without making the verb transitive.
SVO pattern | A clause pattern with a subject, transitive verb and direct object. | Sara opened the door has Sara, opened and the door as S, V and O. | She goes to school is an SVO example because school occurs after the verb. | In goes to school, to school is a prepositional phrase; in traditional clause-pattern accounts it is an adverbial.
SVC pattern | A clause pattern with a subject, linking verb and subject complement. | Ali is tired has tired as the subject complement. | The final element of SVC is always a consonant sound. | Linking verbs connect a subject with an identifying or descriptive complement.
SVA pattern | A clause pattern containing a subject, verb and adverbial, often a required adverbial in strict pattern analyses. | She lives in Lahore uses a location adverbial in a traditional SVA analysis. | The A in SVA means that only a single-word adverb can appear there. | Adverbial is a function that can be realized by a prepositional phrase or another construction.
SVOO pattern | A clause pattern with subject, verb, indirect object and direct object. | Sara gave Ali a book has Ali as indirect object and a book as direct object. | SVOO requires two independent clauses. | The two objects occupy different grammatical relations; prepositional alternatives may express similar participant relations.
SVOC pattern | A clause pattern with subject, verb, object and object complement. | They painted the door green contains an object and a property predicated of it. | Green is a second independent clause in They painted the door green. | Contrast a book in gave Ali a book: it is an object, not a property predicated of Ali.
SVOA pattern | A clause pattern with subject, verb, object and an adverbial required by the construction. | Sara put the book on the shelf contains a required location after the object. | Put the book normally supplies the full intended placement construction without a location. | Required complements and optional adjuncts must be distinguished when assigning strict clause patterns.
Declarative clause | A clause form conventionally associated with making a statement. | The train is late has ordinary declarative form. | A declarative form can never be used to make a request indirectly. | Clause form and speech-act function are related but not identical.
Interrogative clause | A clause form conventionally associated with asking a question. | Is the train late? uses subject-auxiliary inversion. | Every interrogative begins with a wh-word. | Yes/no questions may use inversion without a wh-word; other languages use different resources.
Imperative clause | A clause form conventionally associated with directives. | Close the door uses a base-form verb and an understood addressee. | Every English imperative requires an overt subject before the verb. | Subjects are often unexpressed, though overt you and other special patterns are possible.
Exclamative construction | A grammatical construction conventionally expressing an exclamatory evaluation. | What a lovely day it is! uses a characteristic exclamative pattern. | Every sentence with an exclamation mark must have exclamative syntax. | Punctuation and emotional delivery do not by themselves determine grammatical clause type.
Simple sentence | A sentence containing one independent clause and no subordinate clause. | Ali and Sara laughed and waved contains a single clause with coordinated elements. | A sentence becomes compound whenever it contains the word and. | Coordination of subjects or predicates does not necessarily introduce another independent clause.
Compound sentence | A sentence containing at least two coordinated independent clauses without subordinate clauses in the basic contrast. | Ali cooked, and Sara washed the dishes contains two independent clauses. | A compound sentence consists of one independent and one dependent clause only. | Coordination links units of equivalent syntactic status; subordinate clauses define a different structural relation.
Complex sentence | A sentence with one independent clause and at least one subordinate clause in the basic classification. | Although it rained, we played contains a subordinate clause and a main clause. | A complex sentence must have two independent clauses. | Complexity here concerns clause relations, not vocabulary difficulty or sheer length.
Compound-complex sentence | A sentence with at least two independent clauses and at least one subordinate clause. | When it rained, Ali stayed home, but Sara went out combines both coordination and subordination. | A compound-complex sentence contains no subordinate clause. | Count clause status rather than merely counting verbs or conjunctions.
Structural ambiguity | Multiple interpretations associated with different grammatical groupings or attachments. | I saw the man with binoculars permits an instrument reading and a modifier-of-man reading. | Structural ambiguity always requires one word with two dictionary senses. | Lexical ambiguity involves word senses; structural ambiguity involves alternative structures.
Paraphrase | An alternative expression conveying substantially the same meaning in an intended context. | Sara gave Ali a book and Sara gave a book to Ali describe the same transfer in a suitable context. | Every paraphrase must use exactly the same words in the same order. | Differences in information structure or emphasis can remain despite close propositional equivalence.
Passive voice | A construction in which an argument that is an object in a related active is promoted to subject in a typical English passive. | The door was opened by Ali corresponds to Ali opened the door. | The grammatical subject of a passive must be the semantic agent. | English verbal passives commonly use be plus a past participle; the agent phrase is often optional.
Subject-auxiliary inversion | An English construction placing an auxiliary before the subject in relevant clause types. | Can Sara swim? places can before Sara. | English yes/no questions always move the lexical main verb before the subject. | With no suitable auxiliary, English commonly uses do-support, as in Does Sara swim?
Wh-dependency | A relationship between a wh-expression and the position interpreted as its argument or modifier site. | What did Sara buy? connects what with the understood object of buy. | What in What did Sara buy? has no relation to the thing bought. | Movement analyses model this as a dependency; theoretical accounts differ in implementation.
Do-support | Use of auxiliary do in certain English questions, negatives and emphatic constructions. | Did Ali leave? uses did with the base form leave. | Did Ali left? follows the ordinary standard English question pattern. | The auxiliary bears the finite tense marking, so the following lexical verb appears in its base form.
Parallel structure | Coordination or comparison using appropriately matched grammatical patterns. | She likes reading and swimming coordinates two similarly formed activities. | Parallel structure requires every coordinated expression to have a different grammatical form. | Parallelism supports clarity, though not every surface mismatch is automatically ungrammatical in all contexts.
Loose sentence | A rhetorical sentence arrangement presenting its main clause before added elaboration. | We reached Lahore that evening, tired after the long journey, presents its main assertion early. | A loose sentence must delay its main clause until its very end. | This is a rhetorical organization label, separate from simple/compound/complex clause classification.
Periodic sentence | A rhetorical arrangement that delays completion of the main assertion until later. | After the storm had passed and the road reopened, we finally reached Lahore. | A periodic sentence is defined by having exactly two independent clauses. | Delayed completion creates anticipation; the label concerns rhetorical arrangement.
Inversion | Word order in which elements appear in an order differing from the relevant canonical pattern. | Here comes the bus places the verb before its subject. | Every fronted adverbial necessarily causes subject-verb inversion. | In Lahore, oranges grow fronts an adverbial but leaves the subject before its verb.
Generative grammar | A formal approach aiming to characterize the structures licensed by a linguistic system. | A finite set of structural rules is used to characterize an unbounded set of possible sentences. | Generative means a learner must memorize every possible complete sentence. | Generation is a formal notion about structural descriptions, not a claim that speakers consciously follow written rules.
Transformational grammar | A family of generative models relating structures through transformations. | A historical model relates a question structure to an associated declarative configuration. | Transformational grammar claims that every transformation must preserve all meaning without exception. | Transformations and their interpretation vary across models; the lecture introduces a historical framework.
Deep structure | An underlying syntactic representation in certain earlier transformational models. | A historical analysis distinguishes an underlying representation from an overtly realized structure. | Deep structure is a directly recorded acoustic waveform. | It is model-specific and is not a universal term for meaning in all modern syntactic theories.
Surface structure | A syntactic representation resulting after transformations in certain historical models. | A historical account represents the output order of a passive separately from its underlying representation. | Surface structure simply means the ink on a printed page. | Even surface structure is an abstract syntactic representation rather than a raw sound signal or visual inscription.
Constituency test | A diagnostic operation used as evidence that an expression forms a structural unit. | Replacing the old house with it tests the grouping in The old house collapsed. | A single failed movement test proves that an expression can never be a constituent. | Tests have constructional limits; converging evidence is stronger than relying on one diagnostic alone.
''')
add(7, '''
Lexical semantics | The study of word and morpheme meanings and relations among lexical items. | An analyst compares the meanings of couch and sofa. | Lexical semantics is restricted to meanings of entire paragraphs and excludes words. | Phrasal or compositional semantics instead investigates how larger meanings are built.
Compositional semantics | The study of how complex meanings arise from constituent meanings and their combination. | The analyst explains the difference between Dog bites man and Man bites dog. | Word order and grammatical structure never affect sentence meaning. | The same words can yield different meanings when their structural relations change.
Denotation | The entity or set of entities an expression applies to in a semantic interpretation. | In a suitable model, cat applies to the set of cats. | Denotation is simply any personal emotional association with a word. | Semantic denotations can be defined within models and need not be limited to objects currently visible.
Connotation | Associated social, emotional or evaluative meaning connected with an expression. | Home can evoke warmth or belonging beyond identifying a dwelling. | Connotation is guaranteed to be identical for every speaker in every situation. | Associations can vary across people, cultures and contexts, even when central descriptive meaning is shared.
Extension | The set of entities satisfying an expression in a specified world or situation. | The extension of student at a university includes the individuals enrolled there at the relevant time. | An expression's extension can never change when the world changes. | Membership can vary across worlds and times while the descriptive concept remains relatively stable.
Intension | The descriptive content or rule determining an expression's extension across relevant situations or worlds. | Planet applies according to a concept rather than merely listing currently named objects. | Intension is exactly the same as a person's private emotional reaction to a word. | Intension differs from connotation; in formal semantics it is often modelled as a function from worlds to extensions.
Conceptual meaning | The central descriptive content conventionally associated with an expression. | Needle is understood as a type of thin pointed implement in a relevant sense. | Conceptual meaning consists only of a listener's fear of injections. | Fear is an association; descriptive features identify the intended sense of the expression.
Mental lexicon | A speaker's organized lexical knowledge, including forms, meanings and relevant grammatical properties. | A listener accesses a word's pronunciation and meaning during recognition. | The mental lexicon contains only a list of alphabetically printed pages. | It is a cognitive construct and need not be organized like a printed dictionary.
Semantic feature | A component or property used to represent a meaning contrast. | An analysis distinguishes adult from child using an adulthood feature. | Every lexical meaning can always be exhaustively represented by a tiny fixed feature list. | Feature analysis is useful for selected contrasts but faces difficult cases and theory-dependent choices.
Semantic range | The range of senses or uses associated with a lexical item in a stated analysis. | Run appears in contexts about moving quickly, managing a shop and operating a machine. | A word's semantic range proves that every one of its senses fits every context. | Context and construction constrain which sense is intended.
Selectional restriction | A semantic compatibility constraint or expectation on an expression's arguments. | The table drank tea is anomalous in an ordinary literal interpretation. | A selectional anomaly is automatically a word-order error. | Metaphor, personification and special contexts can support interpretations that violate ordinary literal expectations.
Synonymy | A relation of identical or close meaning in the relevant sense and context. | Couch and sofa are near synonyms for a type of seating. | Near synonyms must be interchangeable in every idiom and social register. | Synonymy is often partial; collocation, register and nuance may differ.
Antonymy | A broad relation of opposition in meaning. | Hot and cold contrast along a temperature dimension. | All antonyms necessarily form a binary opposition with no intermediate cases. | Gradable, complementary and relational oppositions should be distinguished.
Gradable opposition | An opposition organized along a scale admitting intermediate values. | Something can be neither hot nor cold but warm. | Not hot must always entail cold. | Comparatives such as hotter and colder reflect the scalar nature of the contrast.
Complementary opposition | A binary opposition in an idealized domain where one alternative excludes the other. | In an ordinary binary biological classification of an organism, alive contrasts with dead. | Every pair of complementary terms has a normal intermediate point on a degree scale. | The domain and interpretation matter; borderline biological or figurative cases require qualification.
Relational converse | A relation in which opposed terms describe the same situation from different participant perspectives. | If A sold a book to B, B bought the book from A. | Buy and sell describe two unrelated events whenever a transfer occurs. | Converse relations reverse participant perspective rather than describing opposite temperatures on a scale.
Homonymy | Distinct lexical items sharing a form with unrelated meanings. | Bank meaning a financial institution and bank meaning a river edge share a form. | Every pair of related senses of one word must be homonymy. | Distinguishing homonymy from polysemy can require historical and synchronic evidence.
Homophony | Identity of pronunciation between distinct lexical items. | Flour and flower sound alike in many English varieties. | Homophones must always have identical spelling. | Homophony concerns sound; spelling identity is a separate issue.
Homography | Identity of spelling between lexical items or forms, whether or not pronunciation matches. | Lead meaning guide and lead meaning the metal share spelling but differ in pronunciation. | Homographs must always be pronounced identically. | Homography concerns the written form; some homographs are also homophones.
Polysemy | Multiple related senses associated with one lexical item. | Head refers to a body part and also to the leader of an organization. | Polysemy requires unrelated words with coincidentally matching forms. | Relatedness of senses distinguishes the usual polysemy analysis from homonymy, though boundaries can be debated.
Hyponymy | A meaning relation in which one expression denotes a subtype of another. | Rose is a hyponym of flower. | Every flower must be a rose. | If an entity is a rose, it is a flower, but the reverse implication does not generally hold.
Hypernymy | A meaning relation in which one expression names a superordinate category. | Vehicle is a hypernym of bus. | Bus is more general than vehicle in the ordinary classification. | A hypernym includes the subtype in its extension; its descriptive content is usually less specific.
Co-hyponymy | A relation between subtypes sharing a superordinate category. | Bus and car are co-hyponyms under vehicle. | Co-hyponyms must always be synonyms. | Shared category membership does not make the subtypes identical in meaning.
Meronymy | A part-whole lexical relation. | Wheel is a meronym of car in the relevant part-whole sense. | A wheel is a kind of car in the same way that a bus is a kind of vehicle. | Meronymy concerns parts; hyponymy concerns subtypes, so the relations are different.
Metonymy | Reference through a salient association or contiguity between entities. | The White House announced a decision uses a building name for an institution or its officials. | Metonymy always depends on a comparison of similar shapes. | Association such as institution-for-people distinguishes it from resemblance-based metaphor.
Metaphor | Interpretation of one domain or entity through another based on a perceived mapping or comparison. | Calling a fierce competitor a tiger attributes qualities without literally identifying a species. | Every metaphor is an ordinary literal claim that two entities are biologically identical. | Metaphorical interpretation draws on context and conceptual associations.
Prototype | A particularly representative exemplar of a category for a person or community. | A robin may be judged a more typical bird than a penguin in some communities. | A less typical bird is therefore not a member of the bird category. | Typicality is graded and culturally variable; category membership and typicality are distinct.
Lexical ambiguity | Availability of more than one interpretation due to the lexical senses or identities of a form. | I waited at the bank leaves open a financial or river-side interpretation without further context. | Lexical ambiguity must always come from alternative phrase attachments. | Context can disambiguate lexical items, while attachment ambiguity concerns structure.
Agent | A semantic role typically associated with an intentional initiator of an action. | Ali deliberately opened the door assigns Ali the agent role. | The agent must always be the grammatical subject, even in a passive. | In The door was opened by Ali, Ali remains agent while the door is subject.
Theme | A semantic role for an entity undergoing movement, change or a relevant state in a given analysis. | The book moves in Sara carried the book upstairs. | Theme always means the intentional human initiator of an event. | Some frameworks distinguish patient from theme more narrowly; role inventories vary.
Experiencer | A semantic role for a participant who perceives, feels or has a mental state. | Sara is the experiencer in Sara fears spiders. | An experiencer must intentionally cause the emotion experienced. | Experiencers need not control their state and are distinct from intentional agents.
Instrument | A semantic role for the means used to carry out an action. | A key is the instrument in Ali opened the door with a key. | Instrument refers to the destination of a movement event. | A participant used as a means differs from an agent initiating the action.
Location | A semantic role identifying where a state or event occurs. | In Lahore supplies location in Sara worked in Lahore. | Location necessarily describes the point of origin of every movement. | Source and goal concern origin and destination, while location concerns where something is situated.
Source and goal | Semantic roles identifying a movement's origin and destination respectively. | From Lahore is source and to Karachi is goal in The parcel travelled from Lahore to Karachi. | Source and goal always identify the same endpoint by definition. | The roles distinguish direction even when neither expression is the grammatical subject.
Possessor and cause | Semantic roles identifying ownership and nonintentional causal contribution, respectively. | Ali has a bicycle assigns a possessor; The wind broke the branch assigns a natural cause. | A natural force must be an intentional human agent whenever it causes a change. | Cause is often called causer; some inventories include natural forces under a broader agent label.
Entailment | A relation where the truth of one proposition guarantees another's truth under the intended interpretation. | Sara owns a red car entails that Sara owns a car. | An entailment may be cancelled while keeping the original proposition true in the same interpretation. | Entailment is a truth-based relation, unlike a defeasible conversational suggestion.
Contradiction | Incompatibility between propositions that cannot both be true in the same relevant interpretation. | Ali is asleep and Ali is not asleep conflict when time and intended sense are held fixed. | Any two sentences with different words must contradict one another. | Hold reference, time and meaning constant before diagnosing a contradiction.
Tautology | A proposition true by its logical form in the intended classical analysis. | It is raining or it is not raining exemplifies excluded middle in classical logic. | A tautology is defined as an empirical weather prediction that may turn out false. | Logical truth differs from ordinary factual truth; pragmatic use can still make a tautology informative in context.
''')
add(8, '''
Speaker meaning | The message or intention a speaker communicates in a particular use. | A teacher says It is noisy here to encourage the class to quieten down. | Speaker meaning must always equal the dictionary meaning of each isolated word. | Interpreting an intention uses the utterance plus evidence about context; it is not unrestricted mind-reading.
Linguistic context | The surrounding language that supports interpretation of an expression. | In Sara bought a book. She read it, preceding expressions help interpret she and it. | Linguistic context consists only of the physical room where people are standing. | Also called co-text, it differs from physical and wider social circumstances.
Situational context | Relevant features of the communicative situation and shared background. | A warning about a coming car is interpreted differently beside a road than in a delivery discussion. | Situational context excludes who the speaker and hearer are. | Participants, place, time, purposes and assumed background can all affect interpretation.
Common ground | Information participants treat as shared for the purposes of their interaction. | Two colleagues refer to the meeting because both know which scheduled meeting is relevant. | Common ground must consist of facts known by every human being. | Shared assumptions can be local to an interaction and can be mistaken or negotiated.
Conversational implicature | A defeasible meaning inferred from an utterance and conversational reasoning beyond what is explicitly said. | Some students passed may suggest not all, though the speaker can add in fact all did. | Every conversational implicature is a noncancellable logical entailment. | Implicatures depend on contextual assumptions and can often be cancelled without contradiction.
Cooperative principle | Grice's framework for reasoning about contributions appropriate to an accepted conversational purpose. | A listener interprets an apparently indirect reply as relevant to the ongoing discussion. | The cooperative principle guarantees that speakers are friendly and tell the truth. | It is an interpretive framework, not a claim that every actual conversation is morally cooperative.
Quantity maxim | A conversational expectation to provide the amount of information required for the exchange. | Asked for an exact appointment time, a speaker supplies ten thirty rather than only sometime. | Quantity requires speakers to give every fact they know on every occasion. | Appropriate informativeness depends on the purpose of the exchange, not maximum verbosity.
Quality maxim | A conversational expectation to avoid falsehood and claims without adequate evidence. | A researcher qualifies an uncertain result rather than presenting a guess as established fact. | Quality requires making confident claims even when there is no evidence. | Sincerity and evidential support distinguish quality from manner and quantity.
Relation maxim | A conversational expectation that contributions be relevant to the exchange. | Asked where to buy fuel, a speaker mentions a nearby open petrol station. | The relation maxim concerns only whether an utterance is pronounced loudly. | Relevance may be indirect; the listener can infer how a response bears on the current purpose.
Manner maxim | A conversational expectation favoring clarity, order and avoidance of obscurity or unnecessary ambiguity. | A speaker gives ordered directions in clear language. | Manner is the maxim specifically requiring adequate evidence for factual claims. | Manner concerns how something is expressed; evidential truthfulness belongs to quality.
Flouting a maxim | An overt apparent failure to satisfy a maxim that invites an additional interpretation. | During heavy rain, a speaker says Lovely weather! with an obviously ironic delivery. | Flouting is defined as secretly deceiving the listener without any recoverable signal. | A conspicuous mismatch can prompt implicature; flouting differs from covert violation.
Violating a maxim | A covert failure to satisfy a conversational maxim, potentially misleading the listener. | Someone knowingly gives a false alibi while intending it to be believed. | Every violation is a transparent invitation to infer irony. | Whether a maxim is flouted or violated depends on the interaction and the speaker's apparent communicative stance.
Locutionary act | Producing an utterance with a linguistic form and propositional meaning in speech-act analysis. | A speaker utters The gate is open with its ordinary sentence meaning. | Locutionary act means only the psychological effect on a frightened listener. | Locution concerns saying something; illocution concerns the act performed in saying it.
Illocutionary act | The communicative act performed in saying something, such as warning, requesting or promising. | Saying The gate is open functions as a warning that a child may leave. | Illocutionary force is identical to the physical volume of the voice. | The same sentence can perform different acts in different contexts.
Perlocutionary effect | An effect of an utterance on a hearer's actions, feelings or beliefs. | A warning causes a listener to move away from a road. | A warning has no illocutionary force unless the listener actually becomes afraid. | Intended communicative act and actual consequence differ; an appropriate warning may fail to persuade.
Direct speech act | An act whose typical communicative function aligns with the conventional function of its clause form. | Close the door is used straightforwardly to command closure. | A direct speech act must contain a performative verb such as promise. | Directness concerns the relation between form and function, not merely whether a verb is explicitly named.
Indirect speech act | An act communicated through a form conventionally associated with another function. | Could you pass the salt? is used as a request rather than a test of physical ability. | Every interrogative utterance can function only as a request for information. | Listeners infer the intended act from conventions, context and background assumptions.
Explicit performative | An utterance that explicitly names the act it performs in appropriate circumstances. | I promise to return the book tomorrow names the commitment being made. | The past report I promised yesterday necessarily makes a new promise now. | First-person present use is a common pattern, but felicity and actual communicative use also matter.
Felicity conditions | Conditions required for a speech act to be appropriate or successful in a relevant sense. | An institutional declaration succeeds only when authority and procedures are appropriate. | Any institutional formula works equally well when spoken by an unauthorized passer-by. | Conditions differ across acts and may concern authority, circumstances, sincerity and commitment.
Assertive | A speech-act class committing the speaker to the truth of a proposition. | A witness states that the door was locked. | An assertive is defined solely as an attempt to get someone else to act. | Assertions and reports concern truth commitment, while directives seek action from a hearer.
Directive | A speech-act class aiming to get an addressee to do something. | A teacher asks the class to open their books. | A directive necessarily commits the speaker to a future action of their own. | Requests and commands are directives; speaker commitments characterize commissives.
Commissive | A speech-act class committing the speaker to a future course of action. | A student promises to submit the assignment tomorrow. | Commissives only describe the present temperature. | Promises and some offers commit a speaker, subject to the context and the act's conditions.
Expressive | A speech-act class expressing an attitude or psychological state about a situation. | A speaker apologizes for arriving late. | Every expressive changes a person's legal status through institutional authority. | Apologies and congratulations express attitudes; declarations require a different institutional relation.
Declaration | A speech act that changes an institutional state when successfully performed by an appropriate person. | An authorized judge pronounces a sentence in court. | Anyone can successfully declare a defendant legally guilty in any casual conversation. | Institutional authority and procedures distinguish declarations from ordinary assertions about a legal outcome.
Presupposition | Background information presented as already taken for granted in an utterance. | Sara stopped smoking normally presupposes that she used to smoke. | A presupposition must always be true in the actual world. | Speakers can presuppose something false; hearers may challenge or accommodate it.
Presupposition projection | The tendency of some presuppositions to survive under negation or other operators. | Sara did not stop smoking commonly still suggests that Sara smoked before. | Negating an utterance always removes every presupposition associated with it. | Projection is context-sensitive and differs across triggers; survival under negation is a useful diagnostic, not an exceptionless rule.
Factive trigger | An expression conventionally associated with presupposing the truth of its complement. | Sara regrets that she missed the bus normally takes the missed bus as background fact. | Regret normally presupposes that its complement is false. | Compare believe: believing a proposition does not by itself make it factive.
Person deixis | Context-dependent reference anchored to participants in the speech event. | I refers to the current speaker in an ordinary unquoted utterance. | I always refers to the same named individual regardless of who is speaking. | The conventional meaning identifies a participant role; context supplies the particular referent.
Spatial deixis | Context-dependent reference to locations relative to a deictic anchor. | Put it here depends on the speaker's or another established spatial viewpoint. | Here has one unchanging geographical referent in every utterance. | The deictic centre can shift in narrative or reported discourse.
Temporal deixis | Context-dependent reference to time relative to an utterance or other established anchor. | Tomorrow normally denotes the day after the day of speaking. | Tomorrow names the same calendar date in every conversation. | The anchor matters; quotation and narrative can change the relevant reference time.
Social deixis | Linguistic marking that indexes social relationships or status. | Choosing a respectful address form signals the relationship between participants. | Social deixis is defined as the acoustic frequency of a vowel. | Honorifics and address choices can index status without literally describing physical distance.
Discourse deixis | Reference to a portion of discourse as discourse. | That was a strange thing to say uses that to point to a preceding utterance. | Discourse deixis must always refer to an object physically beside the speaker. | It differs from ordinary object reference, though the boundary with anaphora can require careful analysis.
Anaphora | Interpretation of an expression through an antecedent in discourse. | Sara found a key and put it in her bag uses it for the previously mentioned key. | Every pronoun must point to something physically present and can never have a textual antecedent. | Pronouns may be anaphoric or deictic, so pronoun form alone does not establish the reference mechanism.
Accommodation | Updating the shared assumptions to make an utterance's presupposition interpretable when appropriate. | Hearing My sister is visiting, a listener provisionally accepts that the speaker has a sister. | Accommodation requires the hearer to accept every presupposition as unquestionably true. | A hearer may instead challenge an assumption; accommodation is a contextual interpretive move.
''')
